gthr.h: no weak pthread references on wasi-libc.

gthr-posix.h refers to every pthread_* entry point through a __gthrw weakref alias,
so that a program which does not link libpthread degrades to single-threaded instead
of failing to link. That mechanism does two things here and neither is wanted:

  - It is pointless. wasi-libc keeps every pthread_* API in libc -- its libpthread.a
    is an empty 8-byte archive, measured with `llvm-ar t` -- so there is no separate
    library to link and nothing for the reference to be weak against. This is exactly
    the case gthr.h already handles for glibc >= 2.34, with the same reasoning in its
    own comment.

  - It does not assemble. A weakref alias is referenced without GCC ever declaring the
    target symbol external, so ASM_OUTPUT_EXTERNAL is never reached for it and the call
    arrives at llvm-mc with no `.functype`: "symbol pthread_key_create: missing
    .functype", followed by the operand-stack type mismatch that always trails an
    unknown signature. libgcc's emutls.c is the first file to hit it.

So this follows the file's own idiom -- a `__wasi__` arm beside the __MINGW32__ and
__GLIBC_PREREQ ones -- rather than teaching the backend to chase weakref aliases into
the import map.

That backend fix is the alternative and was rejected for now, not forgotten: it would
be the right answer for any target where weak references to undefined functions must be
*callable*, which is a real gap this leaves open. Nothing in the engine or libstdc++
needs it, because 0010 already covers the case libstdc++ actually uses -- a weak
reference that is never called, tested by `weak_undef` in
Tools/wasm_dialect_probe.py. A weak reference that IS called still has no signature.

One consequence, stated because it is invisible otherwise: `__gthread_active_p` now
always answers true, so a std::mutex really locks rather than compiling to nothing.
That is correct for this sysroot -- the pthreads are always present -- and it is what
makes the `std_mutex` probe a measurement rather than a tautology.

## What this subsumes, and the second thing wasi-libc lacks

The first error --enable-threads=posix produced was not the missing `.functype` but
`'pthread_cancel' undeclared here (not in a function)` at gthr-posix.h's
`__gthrw(pthread_cancel)`: wasi-libc has no cancellation support by stated design, its
pthread.h declaring pthread_cancel only under __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream.

That was first fixed on its own, extending the `__BIONIC__` arms gthr-posix.h already
carries at both the __gthrw list and GTHR_ACTIVE_PROXY -- bionic has no pthread_cancel
either. **That patch was then deleted as dead code, which a review caught and which is
worth recording here rather than losing.** Both of its hunks live inside
`#if SUPPORTS_WEAK && GTHREAD_USE_WEAK` regions (gthr-posix.h:116 and :188-299), so
with GTHREAD_USE_WEAK at 0 `__gthrw(pthread_cancel)` expands to nothing and the whole
GTHR_ACTIVE_PROXY block is skipped. Measured rather than argued: with that patch
reverted and this one applied, emutls.o compiles.

So **this patch alone fixes the libgcc build**, and the pthread_cancel repair is only
needed by a configuration nothing here builds. If GTHREAD_USE_WEAK is ever turned back
on for this target -- which would mean the backend had learned to declare weakref
aliases -- gthr-posix.h needs `__wasi__` added beside `__BIONIC__` in those two places
as well, and that is the note this paragraph exists to leave.

--- a/libgcc/gthr.h
+++ b/libgcc/gthr.h
@@ -150,6 +150,18 @@
 #endif
 #endif
 
+#ifdef __wasi__
+/* wasi-libc keeps every pthread_* API in libc, exactly as glibc 2.34 does above:
+   its libpthread.a is an empty archive, so there is no separate library to link
+   and nothing for a weak reference to be weak against.  Leaving the weak
+   references on is also not merely pointless here -- the __gthrw weakref aliases
+   are referenced without GCC ever declaring the target symbol as external, so
+   their calls reach the assembler with no .functype and emutls.c does not
+   assemble.  */
+#undef GTHREAD_USE_WEAK
+#define GTHREAD_USE_WEAK 0
+#endif
+
 #ifndef GTHREAD_USE_WEAK
 #define GTHREAD_USE_WEAK 1
 #endif
